tasks.description:
**Goal:** Command-comma opens a native Settings window with four panes, the Muse Code pane works, and the bridge token travels from the Keychain to `start`.

**Files:**

- Create: `mac/mMac/SettingsView.swift`, `mac/mMac/MuseCodePane.swift`
- Modify: `mac/mMac/MMacApp.swift`, `mac/mMac/AppController.swift`, `mac/mMac/SidebarView.swift`, `mac/mMac/ConversationView.swift`

**Acceptance Criteria:**

- [ ] `MMacApp` has a `Settings` scene. mMac › Settings… (Command-comma) opens it. Its toolbar has four panes in Electron's order: Appearance, Muse Code, MCP servers, Bridges. The last pane shown is kept in `@AppStorage("settingsPane", store: MMacApp.appStorage)`.
- [ ] With no running backend, every pane shows "Settings are available while the Muse backend runs." and nothing else.
- [ ] `AppController.start()` reads `KeychainTokenStore.app` and passes the token to `start(dataFolder:bridgeToken:)`. A Keychain read error does not stop the launch: the backend starts with no token, and the Bridges pane shows the error.
- [ ] `AppController` builds one `SettingsModel` per Workspace and drops it with the Workspace.
- [ ] The Muse Code pane shows, from Electron's `Settings.tsx`:
  - the heading "Muse Code connection";
  - "Muse Code is connected", "Connecting to Muse Code…" or "Muse Code is disconnected";
  - "Version <v>", or the status message;
  - a Reconnect button with the accessibility label "Reconnect to Muse Code";
  - the path in use, and the `fingerprintWarning` when there is one;
  - a "Custom executable path" field with the placeholder "Automatic detection (recommended)";
  - "Browse…" (the M2 open panel, as a sheet on the Settings window) and "Save & reconnect";
  - "New to Muse Code?" with "Open installation guide", which opens `https://dev.meta.ai/docs/muse-code`;
  - "Use automatic detection" when a custom path is saved, as the M1 banner has it. It saves an empty path and reconnects.
- [ ] Clicking the sidebar's connection status opens Settings on the Muse Code pane, as Electron's status pill does. The banner gets "Set up", which does the same.

**Verify:** `xcodebuild build -project mac/mMac.xcodeproj -scheme mMac -derivedDataPath .cache/mmac -quiet` succeeds; Task 15's UI test opens Settings and reads the Muse Code pane; Task 16 checks it live.

